Automation edit integrity
=========================

Ordinary action/condition edits arrive as JSON objects. The runtime evaluates
Action and ConditionGroup instances. Assigning the incoming dictionaries to an
already cached rule breaks later execution even if the update response can be
serialized. Mutating that same cached object before a database lookup or flush
also lets a failed request change the rule visible to the engine.

The edit service now reads the organization-scoped persistent row, restricts
updates to editable fields, normalizes typed/JSON input, and validates an isolated
replacement with the shared record-to-runtime conversion. It writes and flushes
the row before replacing the engine entry. The existing rule object is never
mutated in place, so an already-held execution snapshot is not partially edited.
Missing rows return not-found semantics instead of a phantom success.

PATCH cannot explicitly null required names, trigger definitions, actions,
priority or rate/cooldown values. Omitting these fields preserves them. Optional
descriptions and conditions may still be cleared. Runtime identity, author and
execution counters are not general editable fields.

Qualification scope
-------------------
Regression cases cover normal API-shaped edits, harmless action execution,
condition evaluation, current database definitions, protected fields and invalid
shapes. Database qualification must verify actual committed JSON/runtime shape
agreement, a rejected database write, missing rows and caller rollback against
the exact candidate. Synthetic SQLite probes supplement but never replace the
migrated PostgreSQL release checks. No device or external notification action is
needed for these tests.

Remaining lifecycle limits
--------------------------
Flush is not commit. The caller retains commit/rollback ownership. This change
does not implement commit-aware cache publication: a successful flush followed
by rollback can still leave this worker's cache out of date. Other workers can
also hold older definitions. Authoritative execution-time rule loading,
cross-worker invalidation and in-flight revocation need separate qualification.
A completed external side effect cannot be recalled by disabling a rule.
